    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 110455\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n955 PM PDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  The warming trend peaks Today with an Extreme Heat Warning in\n   effect. Temperatures cool into and through the weekend.\n\n*  Spotty storm activity will be possible in the Sierra Nevada and \n   higher elevations of eastern San Bernardino today. \n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the\n   region Friday through the weekend. With reduced humidities \n   leading to fire weather concerns.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Wednesday\n\nTemperatures and HeatRisk peak today as afternoon highs look to\npeak around 5 degrees above average with most of the desert \nvalleys ranging from the upper 90s to around 105 degrees. Areas to\nthe south of Lake Havasu will exceed 110 and around Death Valley \nlooks to climb above 115 degrees. As such, an Extreme Heat Warning\nis in affect until tonight for Death Valley, the Las Vegas \nValley, Pahrump Valley, Moapa Valley, Colorado River Valley, \nsouthern Mohave County, and southeastern San Bernardino County. \nThe overnight hours will offer some relief, but lows will still \nrange 5 to 10 degrees above average. The upper level pattern \nbegins to push a trough into the area from the Northwest, pushing \nthe high pressure east, and allowing for a cooling trend starting \nFriday. \n\nWeak convection has moved through southern portions of the CWA \nearlier in the day with spotty shower activity moving through\nSierra Nevada near Bishop this after. So far, these showers have\nbeen on the weaker side with some brief lightning activity, but \ndon't look to be impactful.\n\nThe late week pattern change will increase the pressure gradient \nfrom the high pressure to the east to the building trough to the \nnorthwest and result in increasing winds. Friday afternoon will \noffer breezy to gusty winds that look to ease slightly overnight. \nWinds look to become even stronger for  Saturday with widespread \ngusts above 30 mph possible and above 40 mph possible in higher \nterrain and favored gaps and passes. This coupled with very dry \nconditions has increase fire weather concerns, with a Fire Weather \nWatch in effect for Nye, Esmeralda, and portions of Lincoln and \nClark counties for Saturday. Winds look to reduce for Sunday but \nremain breezy. Longer term models are starting to favor breezy\nwinds peaking around 25 mph each afternoon into the first half \nof next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill follow typical directional patterns overnight, and after a \nperiod of variability through the morning, winds will shift to the\nsouth and southwest by early afternoon. Wind speeds will increase\nto 10-15KT with gusts to 20-25KT thereafter through the end of \nthe forecast period. VFR conditions will prevail with passing \nbands of mid-level and high clouds with bases at or above 15kft. \nTemperatures will exceed 100 degrees between 19Z-02Z, reaching a \nhigh of 106 degrees Friday afternoon.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds through Friday\nmorning will remain around 5-10KT and follow typical directional \npatterns. Thereafter through the afternoon and evening, winds \nshift to the south and southwest, increasing to 10-15KT with gusts\nto around 25KT. Exceptions to this include KBIH in the northern \nOwens Valley, where down-valley winds will persist through mid- to\nlate afternoon before shifting to the west with gusts to 20-25KT \nthrough sunset, and KDAG/the western Mojave Desert where winds \nremain westerly through the forecast period with gusts to 20-25KT \nthrough late afternoon, increasing to 30-35KT thereafter through \nthe evening. Otherwise, VFR conditions will prevail, with passing \nbands of clouds with bases at or above 12kft. Isolated \nthunderstorms are expected during the afternoon over portions of \nMohave and Lincoln Counties, though direct impacts at area \nterminals are not anticipated.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...A Fire Weather Watch is in effect from 11AM Saturday \nthrough midnight that night for Nye, Esmeralda, and portions of \nLincoln and Clark counties. Concerns include RH ranging from 5 to \n15% while winds begin to build above 25 mph and gusts approaching 40 \nmph. Winds will last deeper into that night, but humidities will \nbegin to have some recovery by the late that night.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/FIRE WEATHER...Murdock\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
